1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,104
Here's a sentence worth understanding precisely, because it comes up in almost every

2
00:00:05,104 --> 00:00:09,858
capacity conversation: "latency's fine on average, but p95 is rough." Latency is how

3
00:00:09,858 --> 00:00:12,121
long one request takes, start to finish.

4
00:00:12,121 --> 00:00:15,970
Throughput is how many requests the whole system can handle at once.

5
00:00:15,970 --> 00:00:18,120
They sound like the same conversation.

6
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:22,817
They're not, and mixing them up is how a feature that tested beautifully in a quiet

7
00:00:22,817 --> 00:00:26,609
demo turns sluggish the moment real users show up at the same time.

8
00:00:26,609 --> 00:00:31,306
By the end of this lecture you'll know which question to ask when someone shows you

9
00:00:31,306 --> 00:00:36,060
a latency number, and it's rarely "what's the average." Neither of these is a purely

10
00:00:36,060 --> 00:00:39,342
technical concern you can leave to engineering and forget.

11
00:00:39,342 --> 00:00:43,870
Latency shows up directly in whether people finish using your feature or give up

12
00:00:43,870 --> 00:00:44,775
halfway through.

13
00:00:44,775 --> 00:00:49,529
Throughput shows up as a capacity bill and a ceiling on how many users you can serve

14
00:00:49,529 --> 00:00:49,982
at once.

15
00:00:49,982 --> 00:00:54,736
Both are product decisions wearing an engineering vocabulary, and that's exactly why

16
00:00:54,736 --> 00:00:56,999
the vocabulary is worth having yourself.

17
00:00:57,549 --> 00:01:01,472
Keep these separate, because the fix for one can make the other worse.

18
00:01:01,472 --> 00:01:05,507
Latency is what a single person waiting for a single answer experiences.

19
00:01:05,507 --> 00:01:09,766
Make the model faster, trim what's in the context, or stream the response so

20
00:01:09,766 --> 00:01:12,511
something appears immediately, and latency drops.

21
00:01:12,511 --> 00:01:16,938
Throughput is a system property: how many of those requests the whole thing can

22
00:01:16,938 --> 00:01:19,292
process per second before it falls behind.

23
00:01:19,292 --> 00:01:20,749
Here's where they collide.

24
00:01:20,749 --> 00:01:25,064
A common way to raise throughput is batching several requests together before

25
00:01:25,064 --> 00:01:25,960
processing them.

26
00:01:25,960 --> 00:01:30,387
That's great for the system's total capacity, and it often means one particular

27
00:01:30,387 --> 00:01:33,862
request waits slightly longer before its batch is ready to go.

28
00:01:33,862 --> 00:01:38,457
Improve throughput carelessly, and you can quietly make latency worse for everyone

29
00:01:38,457 --> 00:01:39,185
in the batch.

30
00:01:39,185 --> 00:01:43,780
One more distinction worth having: what a user perceives as fast often isn't total

31
00:01:43,780 --> 00:01:46,134
completion time, it's time to first token.

32
00:01:46,134 --> 00:01:50,617
Stream the answer as it's generated, and a five-second response can feel instant

33
00:01:50,617 --> 00:01:54,203
because words started appearing after four hundred milliseconds.

34
00:01:54,203 --> 00:01:58,910
Show nothing until the whole thing is ready, and that same five seconds feels like a

35
00:01:58,910 --> 00:01:59,191
hang.

36
00:01:59,741 --> 00:02:04,282
Here's why engineers won't just tell you the average, and why they're right not to.

37
00:02:04,282 --> 00:02:06,635
Picture a hundred requests to your feature.

38
00:02:06,635 --> 00:02:11,231
Most land under a second, and the average latency you'd calculate across all of them

39
00:02:11,231 --> 00:02:13,420
looks great, comfortably under a second.

40
00:02:13,420 --> 00:02:15,554
But look at the shape, not the average.

41
00:02:15,554 --> 00:02:20,151
A handful of requests, maybe one or two in a hundred, take four, eight, ten seconds.

42
00:02:20,151 --> 00:02:23,926
Average them into the rest and they nearly disappear from the number.

43
00:02:23,926 --> 00:02:27,209
They don't disappear from the person who waited ten seconds.

44
00:02:27,209 --> 00:02:29,179
P95 is the number that catches that.

45
00:02:29,179 --> 00:02:33,721
It's the response time slower than ninety-five percent of requests, and faster than

46
00:02:33,721 --> 00:02:34,979
the worst five percent.

47
00:02:34,979 --> 00:02:36,183
P99 is stricter still.

48
00:02:36,183 --> 00:02:38,208
Neither one is trying to be dramatic.

49
00:02:38,208 --> 00:02:42,421
They're trying to describe the tail an average is specifically built to hide.

50
00:02:42,421 --> 00:02:46,908
This is why "average latency" is close to a red flag on its own when it's the only

51
00:02:46,908 --> 00:02:47,729
number offered.

52
00:02:47,729 --> 00:02:52,270
It's not that averages are wrong, they're answering a genuinely different question:

53
00:02:52,270 --> 00:02:54,349
what does a typical request look like.

54
00:02:54,349 --> 00:02:58,891
P95 answers what an unlucky request looks like, and for a feature people use daily,

55
00:02:58,891 --> 00:03:02,065
someone hits that unlucky case regularly enough to notice.

56
00:03:02,615 --> 00:03:07,630
Say the honest caveat, because a fast demo is the single most common way this number

57
00:03:07,630 --> 00:03:10,079
gets misrepresented, usually by accident.

58
00:03:10,079 --> 00:03:15,035
Run a feature with no one else hitting the system, and latency is close to the best

59
00:03:15,035 --> 00:03:19,991
case every time: no queueing, no contention, nothing waiting on a batch to fill up.

60
00:03:19,991 --> 00:03:25,006
Run the same feature at lunchtime, with real concurrent load, and p95 can move by an

61
00:03:25,006 --> 00:03:29,724
order of magnitude, because throughput and latency were never actually separate

62
00:03:29,724 --> 00:03:30,560
conversations.

63
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:33,485
They just look separate until the system is busy.

64
00:03:33,485 --> 00:03:36,889
Ask for p95 under realistic concurrent load specifically.

65
00:03:36,889 --> 00:03:41,905
A demo number with nobody else in the room is closer to marketing than to a promise.

66
00:03:42,455 --> 00:03:46,884
That's the number worth asking for by name, because "average latency" and "p95

67
00:03:46,884 --> 00:03:51,428
latency" can describe the same system and tell two completely different stories.

68
00:03:51,428 --> 00:03:53,642
The exercise is a habit, not a project.

69
00:03:53,642 --> 00:03:58,299
Every time someone shows you a latency number from here on, notice whether it's an

70
00:03:58,299 --> 00:04:02,843
average or a percentile, and ask for p95 specifically if it isn't already there.

71
00:04:02,843 --> 00:04:07,159
It's a five-second question that heads off a much longer conversation later.

72
00:04:07,709 --> 00:04:09,410
One thing before you move on.

73
00:04:09,410 --> 00:04:12,989
Find your own feature's p95, not the average, the actual p95.

74
00:04:12,989 --> 00:04:16,451
If nobody can produce it, that's worth flagging on its own.

75
00:04:16,451 --> 00:04:21,263
Then ask the harder question: was that number measured under real concurrent load,

76
00:04:21,263 --> 00:04:23,492
or in a quiet moment that flatters it?

77
00:04:23,492 --> 00:04:27,776
The gap between those two answers is usually the gap between the demo and

78
00:04:27,776 --> 00:04:28,421
production.

79
00:04:28,421 --> 00:04:33,232
Next lecture gives you the last piece of this vocabulary: how to read a model card

80
00:04:33,232 --> 00:04:38,161
and a benchmark table the way an engineer already does, without taking either one at

81
00:04:38,161 --> 00:04:38,807
face value.
